WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.360
Hello! If you are new to this channel,&nbsp;
Ms. Coffee Bean sends her warm welcome!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.920
Otherwise, nice to see you again!

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.080
In this video, we will discuss the latest research&nbsp;
trends around the question of where natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:29.840
language processing, or short: NLP, is heading to&nbsp;
and how to really achieve language understanding.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:38.640
“You can’t learn language from the radio”. Why&nbsp;
not? Not even from the l huge amounts of text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:46.480
on the Internet like GPT-3 had access to?
No, not even then. If you want to know why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:53.120
look at our previous video on this, linked below,&nbsp;
where we discuss why a model trained on text only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.120
cannot really understand meaning,&nbsp;
but it can fake it really well.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.400
So, what do we need to learn language?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:08.400
This paper answers this question by setting up a&nbsp;
road map in five levels called the "World Scope".

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.840
The World Scope 1 is: The Corpus. A&nbsp;
long time ago, even before I was born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.800
the people in NLP where trying to&nbsp;
exhaustively annotate clean corpora of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.680
natural language. These corpora were&nbsp;
first used to reconstruct the language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.640
structure (like syntax trees)
then to build word vectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.800
first not contextualized like word2vec&nbsp;
(where Apple and apple are the same),&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.400
or contextualized word embeddings, computed&nbsp;
dynamically depending on the context.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.800
Do these representations really capture meaning?&nbsp;
The core assumption on the corpus level is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.840
context gives the words their meaning.&nbsp;
And it is a very plausible assumption.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:58.160
When someone asks Ms. Coffee&nbsp;
Bean what “coffee” is,&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.200
she can use other words to answer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.800
“a hot drink made from the roasted and&nbsp;
ground seeds (coffee beans)”, and so on.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.480
Word embeddings (like the ones from neural&nbsp;
networks trained on text) are doing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:19.040
same thing, defining words in relationship&nbsp;
to other words: word vectors do not mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.720
anything by themselves, but get their meaning in&nbsp;
relationship to the other words and their vectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:31.280
through vector distances. But what is the&nbsp;
limitation of defining words with other words?

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.960
Let's say, for example, that&nbsp;
we do not know what the word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.480
“seed” means? Then Ms. Coffee Bean can&nbsp;
define it further in terms of other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.160
like “bean” and this you can define again&nbsp;
in term of other words, like “kidney”.&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:52.640
What is a kidney? A bean-shaped organ.&nbsp;
What is a bean? A kidney-shaped seed.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:03:00.400
I hope you see the point: Sooner or later one&nbsp;
runs into big tautologies, when you define word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:07.520
A in terms of word B and word B in terms of word&nbsp;
A. The whole assumption of giving meaning through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.720
context basically floats in the air, where&nbsp;
words help each other up towards meaning,&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.840
but none of these words do&nbsp;
stand themselves on a ground.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:24.400
Then what might a suitable ground for&nbsp;
this whole floating construction be? Think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:30.480
about how much clearer things might become if
I would show you also a picture of coffee while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:38.320
giving a definition about it. But images have no&nbsp;
place in the corpus era of NLP, shooo, go away!

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:45.760
And here we are, at the second World Scope:&nbsp;
The Internet. In the era of the Internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.760
the NLP field has been able to make their&nbsp;
corpora so much bigger through web-crawling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.560
that only the Internet bandwidth,&nbsp;
the GPUs and energy are the limit!

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.600
Huge amounts of text make it much easier to&nbsp;
think that context gives meaning to words:&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.440
Language models like GPT-3 can see&nbsp;
so many words in so many contexts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.520
one might not even notice that the&nbsp;
words do not stand on a real ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.880
and that the language models&nbsp;
are only faking understanding.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.040
But we do notice that the impressive&nbsp;
results of the last months with GPT-3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.680
are not that of a big step towards&nbsp;
natural language understanding: How?

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.200
Even when important steps are being made&nbsp;
in data sizes and neural network sizes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.880
only a comparably little gain is&nbsp;
obtained in simple tasks for humans:&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.320
For example coreference is still a huge problem.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.840
So, the era of the internet has shown us,&nbsp;
that size does matter to solve certain tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.240
but this cannot lead us to the goal&nbsp;
of understanding natural language.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:59.120
What to do? We might consider calling the images&nbsp;
back. Images? Hey, come back here! Please!

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.280
So here we are at World Scope 3: Perception.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.320
Ah, it's multimodal NLP! Ms.&nbsp;
Coffee Bean’s favorite level!

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:15.760
On this level, perception in form of images&nbsp;
and sounds comes to aid the NLP journey towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:21.760
understanding. It should help us in the many&nbsp;
cases where world knowledge together with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:28.240
assumption that we all see and hear the same&nbsp;
world events, makes us leave things unwritten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:35.120
unexplained. If Ms. Coffee Bean says:
“Okay, bye! I go have a sip of coffee now”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.760
we all have a good intuition&nbsp;
why she leaves to drink coffee:

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.600
She is tired, needs the caffeine and likes&nbsp;
the taste of coffee. [OH, that's strange!]

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.320
We know this, without it being&nbsp;
explicitly said. But a purely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:55.120
text-based model could never learn&nbsp;
this if is never written anywhere.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.960
Grounding words in their physical appearance in&nbsp;
images, would surely help a machine translation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:08.000
model to learn that a ball and “o minge” are&nbsp;
the same thing but in different languages.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.320
So we have seen that there is a lot of&nbsp;
motivation for multimodal learning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.720
marry the field of NLP with other amazing fields&nbsp;
like Computer Vision or Audio Signal Processing.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.080
All this has led to great progress on&nbsp;
tasks that require language grounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:28.560
like Visual Question and Answering,&nbsp;
Visual Commonsense Reasoning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:35.440
image captioning, multimodal machine translation,&nbsp;
and so on. For more details exactly on this area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.160
check out Ms. Coffee Bean’s playlist on Multimodal&nbsp;
learning, linked in the description below.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.920
World Scope 3 is great: we gear up&nbsp;
Machine Learning models with perception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:51.600
which helps a lot to observe things.&nbsp;
But can we go even one step further?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.840
What if sometimes we must wait too long&nbsp;
for something to happen to observe it?

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.080
We as humans would then interact&nbsp;
with the system and trigger the event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:05.040
that helps us gather more observation.&nbsp;
Could we also build this into our models?&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.480
Why not? And hereby we are already on&nbsp;
the Level 4 of understanding: Embodiment.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:15.440
This means that by taking actions with a&nbsp;
physical body, the system could trigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:21.280
situations by itself, observe these, and&nbsp;
act again based on the triggered events.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:28.320
We would have a model that would experience&nbsp;
how things act similarly and learn new words.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:34.880
While this sounds awesome, it opens a huge pandora&nbsp;
box of yet unsolved problems: when to explore new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:40.640
situations and when to learn more about the&nbsp;
current one? How to plan a set of actions?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:46.160
How to react, like how to move a muscle?&nbsp;
How to translate these new experiences and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:52.080
interactions into language? Does an experience&nbsp;
even need verbalization or is it of common sense?

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:59.520
Now let’s move on to the top level, to&nbsp;
World Scope 5: The Social World. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.080
in the stages before the model would have&nbsp;
learnt how to acquire data to have a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:10.000
understanding of the world in relation to the&nbsp;
model itself, it still does have any incentive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.200
to communicate or use natural language&nbsp;
that it understands, in the first place!

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:20.560
Interpersonal communication is the most&nbsp;
important usage of natural language and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:26.880
also makes the most important test for AI:&nbsp;
The Turing’s Imitation Game. Only after an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.960
agent has learnt to use language to affect the&nbsp;
world by social interaction it has understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:38.560
not only the meaning of language, but how&nbsp;
to use this meaning to change the world!

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:44.880
But while these five stages stand apparently&nbsp;
separated with research doing well in stages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:51.120
1 and 2, well enough in stage 3 and&nbsp;
only baby steps in steps 4 and 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.240
one has to also understand&nbsp;
how interdependent they are:

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.280
While chatbots are a thing nowadays,&nbsp;
we could argue that we are already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.560
making huge progress in the 5th social&nbsp;
stage. But that is just quite not true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.320
While the system is apparently&nbsp;
socializing with the user,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:14.640
it has no understanding about the communication&nbsp;
situation, about the user and his feelings.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:20.880
Then the chatbot does also not know at all&nbsp;
about what utterances are hurtful in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:27.360
and which ones are hurtful only for specific&nbsp;
persons and playful in other social situations.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.080
The real stage 5 can not be reached&nbsp;
without having modelled the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:35.520
stages of perception and embodiment well enough.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:40.400
So this was it: Ms. Coffee Bean’s shortened&nbsp;
and simplified version of the of this paper’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.600
story. If she made you curious enough, you&nbsp;
can also read the full paper by yourself.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.560
As always, find the link in the description below.

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:49.840
Do not forget to like and subscribe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:10:03.840
and share out the content if you want&nbsp;
to support Ms. Coffee Bean. Okay, bye!

